# What a local run needs that this repository cannot ship.
#
# Copy this file to `.env`, fill in what you have, and source it before the commands in
# README.md's "Running It":
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#     . ./.env
#
# **The `export` on each assignment below is what makes that one line enough**, and it is
# there deliberately. A bare `NAME=value` sourced into a shell is a *shell* variable, which
# a child process does not inherit — so `go run` would not see it, and the file would look
# loaded while doing nothing. The alternative is `set -a; . ./.env; set +a`, which turns on
# automatic exporting around the source; that still works, and the `export`s make it
# unnecessary rather than wrong.
#
# `.env` is git-ignored and this file is the only one of the pair that is ever committed —
# `scripts/check-publication-privacy.sh` refuses any other `.env*` in the tracked tree, and
# scans this one like any other file, so nothing real belongs in the values below.
#
# Nothing here is loaded by a program: no Go or Rust code in this repository reads a `.env`,
# and adding a dependency that did would be a dependency for one line of shell. Sourcing it
# is the whole mechanism, which is also why one shell that has sourced this file can start
# both server processes and why the second variable below needs saying only once.
#
# **It lives in the shell, not in the project.** A new terminal has none of it, and a server
# started from a terminal that never sourced the file is a server with an empty environment
# — which is a supported state, described below, and looks exactly like forgetting.
#
# The client id belongs to the **account service** alone. The registration key is a **shared
# secret both server processes read**: the account service to know what it accepts, the game
# server to present it. The Rust client takes flags and holds no configuration worth hiding.
#
# Leaving either empty is a supported state, not a half-finished one, and neither stops a
# world running. The account service starts with neither and publishes its ticket key; the
# routes that need what is missing answer 503; and a game server with no key says once that
# it will not announce itself, then serves.

# The Discord application's client id, from the Developer Portal.
#
# **Public by construction, and here for a different reason than secrecy.** This is a public
# OAuth client: PKCE stands in for a client secret, and there is no client secret anywhere
# in this service. What this file buys is that an account identifier stays off a command
# line — the publication rules keep one out of every tracked file, message and log, and a
# command carrying one is a command nobody can paste into an issue, a pull request or a CI
# log.
#
# Register `http://127.0.0.1:7780/discord/callback` as the application's redirect URI.
# Exactly that, character for character: the provider matches on the whole string. It is an
# address on the *player's* machine — the client opens a listener there for the length of
# one sign-in — and if you change it, pass the same value to `-discord-redirect-uri`.
#
# `-discord-client-id` is the other way to give this. Giving it in both is refused rather
# than resolved by precedence, so nobody has to remember which one wins.
export VOXELHEIM_DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=

# The key a game server presents to register itself in the account service's server list.
#
# **You invent this one.** It is not issued by anybody: it is a shared secret, and the same
# value goes to both processes — the account service to know what it accepts, the game server
# to present. Printable ASCII with no spaces, between 32 and 256 characters; 32 is what
# `head -c 24 /dev/urandom | base64` produces, and is the length the minimum was chosen
# against. Both ends trim surrounding whitespace, because pasting a key into a file leaves a
# newline behind and an operator would otherwise meet that as a 401 with nothing in any log.
#
# **A real credential, unlike the one above**, and the reason this file exists in the shape it
# does. `-registration-key-file` is the other way to give it and there is deliberately no flag
# that takes the key itself: a flag is visible in `ps` to every user on the machine and lands
# in shell history. As with the client id, giving it in both places is refused.
#
# **Empty is the right answer for a local run**, and costs nothing you were using: the client
# is pointed at `--server 127.0.0.1:7777`, which is an address in no list. What is off is the
# list — the game server announces itself to nobody and says so once at startup, and
# `POST /v1/servers` refuses every request, so the list stays empty. It is still readable, and
# it is read with a ticket rather than with this.
export VOXELHEIM_REGISTRATION_KEY=
